G|0|1|2|0|0|Parity ok (reconcile capped to 1 excerpt vs discrepancy's 1 named-skeptic count). Connectives flag: (a) reconcile connective claims "commentators" read both Matthew's AND Luke's phrasing as idiomatic affirmatives, but the only excerpt given is Matthew-only (TYN on 26:64), leaving the Luke half unsupported; (b) discrepancy connective claims Ehrman "notes...Luke reworded it for theological reasons," implying he presses this row's yes/no-evasiveness tension. Named_skeptic=strawman: Ehrman is real and does list "What did Jesus tell the high priest?" as item 2 of 5 rapid-fire Passion-narrative contradictions in Jesus, Interrupted pp.50-51 (confirmed verbatim via archive.org full text, item JesusInterrupted-RevealingTheHiddenContradictionsInTheBibleAndWhyWeDontKnowAboutThemBartD.Ehrman) - but his actual argument there is that Luke drops Mark's "you will see" clause to avoid an unfulfilled near-term prophecy about the high priest's lifetime, NOT that Luke's reply is evasive; Ehrman himself renders Luke 22:69 as opening with "I am," which undercuts rather than supports the "Matthew/Luke deflect" framing this row needs. Real citation, repurposed to a tension he doesn't press here. Discrepancy_integrity ok (pole is genuinely filled with real, on-topic-adjacent content, not co-opted). Deeper_learning ok: Haley's "Forms of report. Different." pairing of Matt 26:64 "Thou hast said" beside Mark 14:62 "I am," and the closing "fundamental idea is...carefully and distinctly preserved" line, confirmed verbatim on pp.153/156-157 via archive.org djvu.txt for examinationof00hale; link honestly omitted (note documents a search, not a guess).
